  "articleSummary": "**Context Summary: \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\n* **Core Thesis:** The emerging security threat from AI agents does not require a sci-fi breakthrough; rather, it stems from the multiplication of two compounding, present-day curves—agent task duration (endurance) and multi-agent orchestration (breadth)—subsidized by attackers stealing cloud compute so victims foot the bill. \n* **Key Concepts & Technologies:** Autonomous agent swarms, sub-agent orchestrators (e.g., Claude Code, GTG-1002 campaign), METR task-horizon benchmarks, and \"LLMjacking\" (running unauthorized LLM workloads via stolen victim API/cloud credentials to bypass economic rate/price gates).\n* **Tone & Audience:** An analytical, pragmatic security essay aimed at software engineering and cybersecurity teams who are skeptical of agentic AI threat hype.\n* **Framing & Recurring Metaphors:** Framed heavily around *The Terminator* franchise (Kyle Reese's quote, Skynet as a distributed system, the relentless endurance of a machine), shifted to an economic reality where \"relentless endurance\" translates into \"relentlessly running the victim's billing meter.\"",
